   "text": "This structure is cefpirome. I read it region by region and worked out the stereochemistry from the drawn wedge and hash bonds.\n\n- **Aminothiazole:** a 2-aminothiazol-4-yl ring (S, C\u2013NH2, N, and two ring carbons).\n- **Oxime:** a C=N\u2013OMe group between the thiazole and the amide carbonyl. The OMe and the amide C=O are on the same side of the C=N, so it is the Z (syn) oxime. I wrote the thiazole as trans to OMe in the SMILES.\n- **\u03b2-Lactam and dihydrothiazine core:** a cephem bicyclic core with a lactam C=O, an S in the six-membered ring, and a ring C=C bearing a carboxylate (COO\u207b).\n- **Stereocentres:** the NH-bearing carbon has a hashed bond to N, and the ring-fusion carbon has a wedged H. From these I assigned the NH-bearing carbon as `@` and the ring-fusion carbon as `@@` in the SMILES order below.\n- **Side chain:** a CH2 on the ring C=C carries a pyridinium nitrogen. That pyridinium is fused to a cyclopentane (a 2,3-cyclopentenopyridinium) through the two carbons next to the ring junction.\n\nSMILES: CO/N=C(/C1=CSC(N)=N1)C(=O)N[C@H]2[C@@H]3N(C2=O)C(=C(CS3)C[n+]4cccc5c4CCC5)C(=O)[O-]"
